<PubmedArticleSet><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Automated"><PMID Version="1">37075701</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2023</Year><Month>04</Month><Day>21</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>01</Month><Day>27</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">2666-3791</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>4</Volume><Issue>4</Issue><PubDate><Year>2023</Year><Month>Apr</Month><Day>18</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Cell reports. Medicine</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Cell Rep Med</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>A kinome-wide CRISPR screen identifies CK1&#945; as a target to overcome enzalutamide resistance of prostate cancer.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>101015</StartPage><MedlinePgn>101015</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">101015</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.xcrm.2023.101015</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S2666-3791(23)00125-8</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Enzalutamide (ENZA), a second-generation androgen receptor antagonist, has significantly increased progression-free and overall survival of patients with metastatic prostate cancer (PCa). However, resistance remains a prominent obstacle in treatment. Utilizing a kinome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 knockout screen, we identified casein kinase 1&#945; (CK1&#945;) as a therapeutic target to overcome ENZA resistance. Depletion or pharmacologic inhibition of CK1&#945; enhanced ENZA efficacy in ENZA-resistant cells and patient-derived xenografts. Mechanistically, CK1&#945; phosphorylates the serine residue S1270 and modulates the protein abundance of ataxia telangiectasia mutated (ATM), a primary initiator of DNA double-strand break (DSB)-response signaling, which is compromised in ENZA-resistant cells and patients. Inhibition of CK1&#945; stabilizes ATM, resulting in the restoration of DSB signaling, and thus increases ENZA-induced cell death and growth arrest. Our study details a therapeutic approach for ENZA-resistant PCa and characterizes a particular perspective for the function of CK1&#945; in the regulation of DNA-damage response.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>Copyright &#169; 2023 The Author(s). Published by Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Liu</LastName><ForeName>Jinghui</ForeName><Initials>J</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Toxicology and Cancer Biology, University of Kentucky, Lexington, KY 40536, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Zhao</LastName><ForeName>Yue</ForeName><Initials>Y</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Biomedical Informatics, The Ohio State University, Columbus, OH 43210, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>He</LastName><ForeName>Daheng</ForeName><Initials>D</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Markey Cancer Center, University of Kentucky, Lexington, KY 40536, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Jones</LastName><ForeName>Katelyn M</ForeName><Initials>KM</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Toxicology and Cancer Biology, University of Kentucky, Lexington, KY 40536, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Tang</LastName><ForeName>Shan</ForeName><Initials>S</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Biomedical Informatics, The Ohio State University, Columbus, OH 43210, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Allison</LastName><ForeName>Derek B</ForeName><Initials>DB</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Markey Cancer Center, University of Kentucky, Lexington, KY 40536, USA; Department of Pathology and Laboratory Medicine, University of Kentucky, Lexington, KY 40536, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Autho